🐛 Bug
In this piece of code:
  float zero = 0.;
  float one = 1.;
  auto test = torch::tensor(
    {
      zero, one/2.
    },
    {torch::kFloat32});

This will exception with
C++ exception with description "Expected all elements of the tensor to have the same scalar type: Double, but got element of scalar type: Float

The way to fix this is to do one/(float)2. which seems odd.

🐛 Bug
1D convolution is broken for mkldnn tensors and a badly-worded error is thrown for this case.
To Reproduce
import torch

input = torch.randn(2, 3, 10).to_mkldnn()
weight = torch.randn(3, 3, 3).to_mkldnn()
bias = torch.randn(3).to_mkldnn()
output = torch.nn.functional.conv1d(input, weight, bias)
RuntimeError: opaque tensors do not have strides

Expected behavior
Either the correct output is returned for mkldnn tensor inputs or a proper error is thrown indicating the lack of support for 1D convolution with mkldnn tensors.
Additional Context
The problem occurs when trying to view 1D spatial input / weight as 2D. This is done for other backends (e.g. cuDNN) that don't support 1D spatial input directly. However, the mkldnn convolution docs indicate that 1D spatial inputs are supported directly, so it should be an easy fix to avoid the view for the mkldnn case.



